<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Technical notes</h2>

  <h3 id="screen">Why the screen behaves the way it does.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    The panel is a 1.54&Prime; e-paper, 200&times;200 pixels. It keeps its last
    image with the rails down and draws nothing to keep it there, which is what
    makes the parked wordmark an indicator and a blank screen the right thing to
    store.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    In flight, every frame is a partial refresh. The full waveform, the one that
    flashes the panel black and white for a second and a half, is spent twice in
    a session: at power on, and at power off. A page change goes through a
    single black wipe instead, and the alarm&rsquo;s one flash a second is the
    panel&rsquo;s own floor rather than a rate we picked. Faster is possible,
    and a large black region toggled that hard marks the glass for good.
  </p>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>Partial refresh on every frame</li>
    <li>The full waveform twice a session</li>
    <li>A page change is one black wipe, a third of a second</li>
    <li>A frame identical to the last is never sent</li>
    <li>Left alone above 50&nbsp;&deg;C, its maker&rsquo;s limit</li>
    <li>Parked white at the cutoff, never mid-picture</li>
  </ul>
</section>

